tributed.\n3. The variances of the errors are constant (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nResolution IV designs are applicable when researchers need to estimate main effects and two-factor interactions without the risk of confounding. They are particularly useful in situations where resources are limited, and a full factorial design is impractical. Selection criteria include the number of factors, the desired level of interaction to be estimated, and the available experimental runs.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of data from a Resolution IV design typically involves the use of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to assess the significance of the main effects and two-factor interactions. The model is fitted to the data, and hypothesis tests are conducted to determine which effects are statistically significant.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe primary advantages of Resolution IV designs include:\n- Clear estimation of main effects without confounding with two-factor interactions.\n- Efficient use of resources by allowing for a reduced number of experimental runs.\n- Flexibility in exploring interactions while maintaining clarity in the interpretation of results.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nDespite their strengths, Resolution IV designs have limitations:\n- Two-factor interactions may still be aliased with each other, which can complicate interpretation.\n- The assumption that higher-order interactions are negligible may not hold in all situations, potentially leading to misleading conclusions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include Resolution III and Resolution V designs, which offer different balances between clarity and the ability to estimate interactions. Full factorial designs serve as an alternative when resources allow for complete estimation of all effects.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing a Resolution IV design, researche